NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"issue-1939-offline-admission-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1939 offline admission review"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reviewed-state-and-contract","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed state and contract"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source review: "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1939","children":[{"type":"text","value":"issue #1939"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c0d123b86aeb2942d0a2a70a050bf10a3b3dda48"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"f1ae370853b3d3e0f5e0e770ef4dbb5866239b81"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Owner: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An action selected for offline execution must not fulfill without either an\nadmitted outbox record or a provider call. This repair chooses rejection and\nrollback when leadership is lost before admission. A later action invocation\nwhile offline execution is disabled selects the online-only path instead.\nThe manual offline transaction path uses the same admission guard."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lossless-issue-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lossless issue review"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source claim"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verdict and action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leadership loss before admission can fulfill with no outbox record or provider call and an absent Collection row."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed on base; fixed now."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Admission safety relation in the leadership oracle."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":" can revoke leadership after offline selection; the old guard calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveTransaction()"},{"type":"text","value":" after an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed by controlled path and source; fixed now."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synchronous and microtask loss schedules retained in the oracle."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose rejection/rollback or durable handoff, checking public settlement."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejection/rollback chosen and verified; fixed now."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NonRetriableError"},{"type":"text","value":" contract and offline guide."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Already durable replay coverage misses selection-to-admission loss; extend the primary oracle, prove RED/GREEN, and record scope."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed gap; fixed now."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounded action matrix, manual path, and coverage map."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guide's online-only statement applies to actions starting disabled, not an action already selected for offline execution."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed; fixed now."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Corrected path-selection and pre-admission failure wording."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R6"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR #1938 changed documentation only; RFC #1659 owns broader admission policy."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed provenance; deferred context."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coverage map retains the broader boundary and needed witnesses."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The raw issue contains these six claims and no separate comments at review.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git show --stat c0d123b8"},{"type":"text","value":" confirms that PR #1938 did not change production\ncode. The issue's proposed durable handoff remains a valid alternative design,\nbut the selected rejection policy needs no new queue or ownership state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"red-green-and-scope","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RED, GREEN, and scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The issue's original action history failed before the fix with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['fulfilled', 0, 0]"},{"type":"text","value":": settled outcome, durable outbox count, provider-call\ncount. The retained-leader and before-invocation controls passed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After adding the microtask and manual paths, a temporary restoration of the\noriginal guard caused three assertion failures at the intended checkpoints:\nloss during "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":", loss just after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":", and manual loss before\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":". Two adjacent action controls passed. These are assertion kills,\nnot timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths. The original guard was\nremoved again. All five admission cases then passed, as did the full package\nsuite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file Prettier, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed against the reviewed executable head. The commit\nhook ran ESLint on the changed TypeScript files."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bounded action grammar has four schedules: retained leadership, loss\nbefore invocation, loss during "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":", and microtask loss just afterward.\nIt uses one Collection row and an immediate fake store. The direct manual\nhistory loses leadership after optimistic mutation and before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe driver waits for public settlement or a completed outbox write, then\ncompares promise outcome, durable records, provider calls, and public row.\nThe loss cases require a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NonRetriableError"},{"type":"text","value":" rejection, no durable record or\nprovider call, and rollback. The controls require provider execution and a\nfulfilled transaction. Removing either loss schedule would let a check placed\nonly at the other boundary survive. An asynchronous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":" return is\nexcluded by the public API; neither this fixture nor the fake store models\nnative storage, leader regain, or a write held across leadership loss."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-guide-audit-orc-001-through-orc-012","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide audit (ORC-001 through ORC-012)"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Issue #1939 states the no-false-success law; the offline guide and transaction settlement owner state promise and rollback behavior. The bounded limits are above and in the coverage map."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Expected results come from settlement, durability, and leadership laws. The test does not import the admission classifier to compute them."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003 visible responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The owner header states contract and limits; the local relation, four action histories plus manual history, real entry points, and public comparisons are together."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004 generated grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered: this addition is bounded enumeration, not a generated-history property. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion for its finite grammar are stated above. Existing generated properties are unchanged."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005 path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOfflineAction()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOfflineTransaction()"},{"type":"text","value":" run through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistTransaction()"},{"type":"text","value":". The original mutant reached the public settlement and outbox/provider comparisons."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The original guard was a plausible wrong design and produced three assertion failures while controls passed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 fixed/random replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered: the added fixed matrix is not an important generated property. Existing leadership properties retain their fixed/random campaigns and replay interface."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008 stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered: the new relation adds no reference-model state."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009 vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The test-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loss"},{"type":"text","value":" axis names callback timing. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":", admission, outbox, leadership, and settlement retain production meanings."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010 failure and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atOracleCheckpoint()"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds hangs. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanupOfflineOracle()"},{"type":"text","value":" retains primary failures and reports cleanup separately; the mutant failed at assertions without cleanup replacing them."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011 second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered: no shared semantic fault requiring a second reference formulation was identified. The manual API is an adjacent production path, not a copied reference model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record ties the audit and RED/GREEN evidence to the exact executable commit above. It was added after that code commit; no executable file changed afterward."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repair covers these action and manual selection-to-admission histories.\nThe leadership oracle still needs held outbox writes crossed with leadership\nloss and regain, with settlement checked after the write. The IndexedDB\nwrite-settlement owner needs a browser-host composition of that timing with\nthe adapter's transaction-completion boundary. The coverage map names both\nowners."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pr-1966-follow-up-admission-and-provider-checkpoints","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR #1966 follow-up: admission and provider checkpoints"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External review: two test-assertion findings supplied for PR #1966."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Starting head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bf742b1b7"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable head after repair: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2579ac0acf70465a3902a8317ff241129b9b550e"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Owner: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review claim"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verdict and action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F0"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production rejection and rollback path is sound."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed; already fixed before this review. The original-guard mutant and five admission cases are recorded above."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the public settlement, outbox, provider-call, and optimistic-row checks in the leadership oracle."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The retained-leader provider-call count can run after storage becomes visible but before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executor.execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" starts."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed; fixed in the reviewed executable head."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle now separates the stored-record checkpoint from transaction settlement."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"F2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comparing the manual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted.promise"},{"type":"text","value":" errors by identity overconstrains the contract; check only their class."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refuted as a test flaw; retain the identity assertion."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly owns the same-instance error law for the underlying transaction. The offline manual path checks that its wrapper preserves it."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A controlled storage wrapper wrote the retained-leader record, signaled that it\nwas visible, and held its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set()"},{"type":"text","value":" completion. On the starting code, the old\nprovider-count assertion failed at its original checkpoint: expected one call,\nobserved zero. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup failure.\nAfter the repair, the same held-write case observed a pending caller, one stored\nrecord, and zero provider calls. It then released the write, awaited public\nsettlement, and observed one provider call and fulfillment. Clearing the\nprovider-call recorder after settlement made the moved assertion fail, proving\nthat the later check still rejects a missing call. The recorder corruption\nwas removed; the controlled hold remains in the oracle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted.promise"},{"type":"text","value":" identity law is explicit in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and in the core transaction's original\nerror rethrow. The offline wrapper rethrows the same error. A temporary wrapper\nthat threw a fresh "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NonRetriableError"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same message made the manual\noracle's identity assertion fail at the intended checkpoint. Replacing that\nassertion with the proposed class-only check let the mutant pass. Both temporary\nchanges were removed; the core identity test passed on this branch. The review\ncorrectly spotted a dependence on identity, but that dependence protects an\nestablished behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The changed executable owner passed its focused held-write case and the full\noffline package suite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file\nPrettier, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":", and the commit hook's ESLint task passed. The\naddition retains the admission law and bounded leadership-loss scope. In particular,\nORC-005 now names the stored-record and settled-call checkpoints separately;\nORC-006 has the controlled old-order failure and missing-call control;\nORC-010 releases the held write during cleanup; ORC-012 records the exact\nexecutable head. The new hold keeps leadership throughout the write. Leadership\nloss or regain across a held write and the IndexedDB browser composition remain\nwith the owners in the coverage map; this review does not close those cells."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"issue-1939-offline-admission-review","text":"Issue #1939 offline admission review","level":1},{"id":"reviewed-state-and-contract","text":"Reviewed state and contract","level":2},{"id":"lossless-issue-review","text":"Lossless issue review","level":2},{"id":"red-green-and-scope","text":"RED, GREEN, and scope","level":2},{"id":"oracle-guide-audit-orc-001-through-orc-012","text":"Oracle guide audit (ORC-001 through ORC-012)","level":2},{"id":"pr-1966-follow-up-admission-and-provider-checkpoints","text":"PR #1966 follow-up: admission and provider checkpoints","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Issue #1939 offline admission review\n\n## Reviewed state and contract\n\n- Source review: [issue #1939](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1939).\n- Base: `c0d123b86aeb2942d0a2a70a050bf10a3b3dda48` (`origin/main`).\n- Reviewed executable head: `f1ae370853b3d3e0f5e0e770ef4dbb5866239b81`.\n- Owner: `packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts`.\n\nAn action selected for offline execution must not fulfill without either an\nadmitted outbox record or a provider call. This repair chooses rejection and\nrollback when leadership is lost before admission. A later action invocation\nwhile offline execution is disabled selects the online-only path instead.\nThe manual offline transaction path uses the same admission guard.\n\n## Lossless issue review\n\n| ID | Source claim | Verdict and action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R1 | Leadership loss before admission can fulfill with no outbox record or provider call and an absent Collection row. | Confirmed on base; fixed now. | Admission safety relation in the leadership oracle. |\n| R2 | `onMutate` can revoke leadership after offline selection; the old guard calls `resolveTransaction()` after an `await`. | Confirmed by controlled path and source; fixed now. | Synchronous and microtask loss schedules retained in the oracle. |\n| R3 | Choose rejection/rollback or durable handoff, checking public settlement. | Rejection/rollback chosen and verified; fixed now. | `NonRetriableError` contract and offline guide. |\n| R4 | Already durable replay coverage misses selection-to-admission loss; extend the primary oracle, prove RED/GREEN, and record scope. | Confirmed gap; fixed now. | Bounded action matrix, manual path, and coverage map. |\n| R5 | The guide's online-only statement applies to actions starting disabled, not an action already selected for offline execution. | Confirmed; fixed now. | Corrected path-selection and pre-admission failure wording. |\n| R6 | PR #1938 changed documentation only; RFC #1659 owns broader admission policy. | Confirmed provenance; deferred context. | Coverage map retains the broader boundary and needed witnesses. |\n\nThe raw issue contains these six claims and no separate comments at review.\n`git show --stat c0d123b8` confirms that PR #1938 did not change production\ncode. The issue's proposed durable handoff remains a valid alternative design,\nbut the selected rejection policy needs no new queue or ownership state.\n\n## RED, GREEN, and scope\n\nThe issue's original action history failed before the fix with\n`['fulfilled', 0, 0]`: settled outcome, durable outbox count, provider-call\ncount. The retained-leader and before-invocation controls passed.\n\nAfter adding the microtask and manual paths, a temporary restoration of the\noriginal guard caused three assertion failures at the intended checkpoints:\nloss during `onMutate`, loss just after `onMutate`, and manual loss before\n`commit()`. Two adjacent action controls passed. These are assertion kills,\nnot timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths. The original guard was\nremoved again. All five admission cases then passed, as did the full package\nsuite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file Prettier, and\n`git diff --check` passed against the reviewed executable head. The commit\nhook ran ESLint on the changed TypeScript files.\n\nThe bounded action grammar has four schedules: retained leadership, loss\nbefore invocation, loss during `onMutate`, and microtask loss just afterward.\nIt uses one Collection row and an immediate fake store. The direct manual\nhistory loses leadership after optimistic mutation and before `commit()`.\nThe driver waits for public settlement or a completed outbox write, then\ncompares promise outcome, durable records, provider calls, and public row.\nThe loss cases require a `NonRetriableError` rejection, no durable record or\nprovider call, and rollback. The controls require provider execution and a\nfulfilled transaction. Removing either loss schedule would let a check placed\nonly at the other boundary survive. An asynchronous `onMutate` return is\nexcluded by the public API; neither this fixture nor the fake store models\nnative storage, leader regain, or a write held across leadership loss.\n\n## Oracle guide audit (ORC-001 through ORC-012)\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 authority and limits | Pass. Issue #1939 states the no-false-success law; the offline guide and transaction settlement owner state promise and rollback behavior. The bounded limits are above and in the coverage map. |\n| ORC-002 independent judgment | Pass. Expected results come from settlement, durability, and leadership laws. The test does not import the admission classifier to compute them. |\n| ORC-003 visible responsibilities | Pass. The owner header states contract and limits; the local relation, four action histories plus manual history, real entry points, and public comparisons are together. |\n| ORC-004 generated grammar controls | Not triggered: this addition is bounded enumeration, not a generated-history property. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion for its finite grammar are stated above. Existing generated properties are unchanged. |\n| ORC-005 path and observation | Pass. `createOfflineAction()` and `createOfflineTransaction()` run through `persistTransaction()`. The original mutant reached the public settlement and outbox/provider comparisons. |\n| ORC-006 checker calibration | Pass. The original guard was a plausible wrong design and produced three assertion failures while controls passed. |\n| ORC-007 fixed/random replay | Not triggered: the added fixed matrix is not an important generated property. Existing leadership properties retain their fixed/random campaigns and replay interface. |\n| ORC-008 stateful-model minimality | Not triggered: the new relation adds no reference-model state. |\n| ORC-009 vocabulary mapping | Pass. The test-only `loss` axis names callback timing. `onMutate`, admission, outbox, leadership, and settlement retain production meanings. |\n| ORC-010 failure and cleanup | Pass. `atOracleCheckpoint()` bounds hangs. `cleanupOfflineOracle()` retains primary failures and reports cleanup separately; the mutant failed at assertions without cleanup replacing them. |\n| ORC-011 second formulation | Not triggered: no shared semantic fault requiring a second reference formulation was identified. The manual API is an adjacent production path, not a copied reference model. |\n| ORC-012 review evidence | This record ties the audit and RED/GREEN evidence to the exact executable commit above. It was added after that code commit; no executable file changed afterward. |\n\nThe repair covers these action and manual selection-to-admission histories.\nThe leadership oracle still needs held outbox writes crossed with leadership\nloss and regain, with settlement checked after the write. The IndexedDB\nwrite-settlement owner needs a browser-host composition of that timing with\nthe adapter's transaction-completion boundary. The coverage map names both\nowners.\n\n## PR #1966 follow-up: admission and provider checkpoints\n\n- External review: two test-assertion findings supplied for PR #1966.\n- Starting head: `bf742b1b7`.\n- Reviewed executable head after repair: `2579ac0acf70465a3902a8317ff241129b9b550e`.\n- Owner: `packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts`.\n\n| ID | Review claim | Verdict and action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F0 | The production rejection and rollback path is sound. | Confirmed; already fixed before this review. The original-guard mutant and five admission cases are recorded above. | Keep the public settlement, outbox, provider-call, and optimistic-row checks in the leadership oracle. |\n| F1 | The retained-leader provider-call count can run after storage becomes visible but before `executor.execute()` starts. | Confirmed; fixed in the reviewed executable head. | The oracle now separates the stored-record checkpoint from transaction settlement. |\n| F2 | Comparing the manual `commit()` and `isPersisted.promise` errors by identity overconstrains the contract; check only their class. | Refuted as a test flaw; retain the identity assertion. | `packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts` explicitly owns the same-instance error law for the underlying transaction. The offline manual path checks that its wrapper preserves it. |\n\nA controlled storage wrapper wrote the retained-leader record, signaled that it\nwas visible, and held its `set()` completion. On the starting code, the old\nprovider-count assertion failed at its original checkpoint: expected one call,\nobserved zero. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup failure.\nAfter the repair, the same held-write case observed a pending caller, one stored\nrecord, and zero provider calls. It then released the write, awaited public\nsettlement, and observed one provider call and fulfillment. Clearing the\nprovider-call recorder after settlement made the moved assertion fail, proving\nthat the later check still rejects a missing call. The recorder corruption\nwas removed; the controlled hold remains in the oracle.\n\nThe `commit()`/`isPersisted.promise` identity law is explicit in\n`packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts` and in the core transaction's original\nerror rethrow. The offline wrapper rethrows the same error. A temporary wrapper\nthat threw a fresh `NonRetriableError` with the same message made the manual\noracle's identity assertion fail at the intended checkpoint. Replacing that\nassertion with the proposed class-only check let the mutant pass. Both temporary\nchanges were removed; the core identity test passed on this branch. The review\ncorrectly spotted a dependence on identity, but that dependence protects an\nestablished behavior.\n\nThe changed executable owner passed its focused held-write case and the full\noffline package suite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file\nPrettier, `git diff --check`, and the commit hook's ESLint task passed. The\naddition retains the admission law and bounded leadership-loss scope. In particular,\nORC-005 now names the stored-record and settled-call checkpoints separately;\nORC-006 has the controlled old-order failure and missing-call control;\nORC-010 releases the held write during cleanup; ORC-012 records the exact\nexecutable head. The new hold keeps leadership throughout the write. Leadership\nloss or regain across a held write and the IndexedDB browser composition remain\nwith the owners in the coverage map; this review does not close those cells.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Issue #1939 offline admission review\n\n## Reviewed state and contract\n\n- Source review: [issue #1939](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1939).\n- Base: `c0d123b86aeb2942d0a2a70a050bf10a3b3dda48` (`origin/main`).\n- Reviewed executable head: `f1ae370853b3d3e0f5e0e770ef4dbb5866239b81`.\n- Owner: `packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts`.\n\nAn action selected for offline execution must not fulfill without either an\nadmitted outbox record or a provider call. This repair chooses rejection and\nrollback when leadership is lost before admission. A later action invocation\nwhile offline execution is disabled selects the online-only path instead.\nThe manual offline transaction path uses the same admission guard.\n\n## Lossless issue review\n\n| ID | Source claim | Verdict and action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R1 | Leadership loss before admission can fulfill with no outbox record or provider call and an absent Collection row. | Confirmed on base; fixed now. | Admission safety relation in the leadership oracle. |\n| R2 | `onMutate` can revoke leadership after offline selection; the old guard calls `resolveTransaction()` after an `await`. | Confirmed by controlled path and source; fixed now. | Synchronous and microtask loss schedules retained in the oracle. |\n| R3 | Choose rejection/rollback or durable handoff, checking public settlement. | Rejection/rollback chosen and verified; fixed now. | `NonRetriableError` contract and offline guide. |\n| R4 | Already durable replay coverage misses selection-to-admission loss; extend the primary oracle, prove RED/GREEN, and record scope. | Confirmed gap; fixed now. | Bounded action matrix, manual path, and coverage map. |\n| R5 | The guide's online-only statement applies to actions starting disabled, not an action already selected for offline execution. | Confirmed; fixed now. | Corrected path-selection and pre-admission failure wording. |\n| R6 | PR #1938 changed documentation only; RFC #1659 owns broader admission policy. | Confirmed provenance; deferred context. | Coverage map retains the broader boundary and needed witnesses. |\n\nThe raw issue contains these six claims and no separate comments at review.\n`git show --stat c0d123b8` confirms that PR #1938 did not change production\ncode. The issue's proposed durable handoff remains a valid alternative design,\nbut the selected rejection policy needs no new queue or ownership state.\n\n## RED, GREEN, and scope\n\nThe issue's original action history failed before the fix with\n`['fulfilled', 0, 0]`: settled outcome, durable outbox count, provider-call\ncount. The retained-leader and before-invocation controls passed.\n\nAfter adding the microtask and manual paths, a temporary restoration of the\noriginal guard caused three assertion failures at the intended checkpoints:\nloss during `onMutate`, loss just after `onMutate`, and manual loss before\n`commit()`. Two adjacent action controls passed. These are assertion kills,\nnot timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths. The original guard was\nremoved again. All five admission cases then passed, as did the full package\nsuite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file Prettier, and\n`git diff --check` passed against the reviewed executable head. The commit\nhook ran ESLint on the changed TypeScript files.\n\nThe bounded action grammar has four schedules: retained leadership, loss\nbefore invocation, loss during `onMutate`, and microtask loss just afterward.\nIt uses one Collection row and an immediate fake store. The direct manual\nhistory loses leadership after optimistic mutation and before `commit()`.\nThe driver waits for public settlement or a completed outbox write, then\ncompares promise outcome, durable records, provider calls, and public row.\nThe loss cases require a `NonRetriableError` rejection, no durable record or\nprovider call, and rollback. The controls require provider execution and a\nfulfilled transaction. Removing either loss schedule would let a check placed\nonly at the other boundary survive. An asynchronous `onMutate` return is\nexcluded by the public API; neither this fixture nor the fake store models\nnative storage, leader regain, or a write held across leadership loss.\n\n## Oracle guide audit (ORC-001 through ORC-012)\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 authority and limits | Pass. Issue #1939 states the no-false-success law; the offline guide and transaction settlement owner state promise and rollback behavior. The bounded limits are above and in the coverage map. |\n| ORC-002 independent judgment | Pass. Expected results come from settlement, durability, and leadership laws. The test does not import the admission classifier to compute them. |\n| ORC-003 visible responsibilities | Pass. The owner header states contract and limits; the local relation, four action histories plus manual history, real entry points, and public comparisons are together. |\n| ORC-004 generated grammar controls | Not triggered: this addition is bounded enumeration, not a generated-history property. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion for its finite grammar are stated above. Existing generated properties are unchanged. |\n| ORC-005 path and observation | Pass. `createOfflineAction()` and `createOfflineTransaction()` run through `persistTransaction()`. The original mutant reached the public settlement and outbox/provider comparisons. |\n| ORC-006 checker calibration | Pass. The original guard was a plausible wrong design and produced three assertion failures while controls passed. |\n| ORC-007 fixed/random replay | Not triggered: the added fixed matrix is not an important generated property. Existing leadership properties retain their fixed/random campaigns and replay interface. |\n| ORC-008 stateful-model minimality | Not triggered: the new relation adds no reference-model state. |\n| ORC-009 vocabulary mapping | Pass. The test-only `loss` axis names callback timing. `onMutate`, admission, outbox, leadership, and settlement retain production meanings. |\n| ORC-010 failure and cleanup | Pass. `atOracleCheckpoint()` bounds hangs. `cleanupOfflineOracle()` retains primary failures and reports cleanup separately; the mutant failed at assertions without cleanup replacing them. |\n| ORC-011 second formulation | Not triggered: no shared semantic fault requiring a second reference formulation was identified. The manual API is an adjacent production path, not a copied reference model. |\n| ORC-012 review evidence | This record ties the audit and RED/GREEN evidence to the exact executable commit above. It was added after that code commit; no executable file changed afterward. |\n\nThe repair covers these action and manual selection-to-admission histories.\nThe leadership oracle still needs held outbox writes crossed with leadership\nloss and regain, with settlement checked after the write. The IndexedDB\nwrite-settlement owner needs a browser-host composition of that timing with\nthe adapter's transaction-completion boundary. The coverage map names both\nowners.\n\n## PR #1966 follow-up: admission and provider checkpoints\n\n- External review: two test-assertion findings supplied for PR #1966.\n- Starting head: `bf742b1b7`.\n- Reviewed executable head after repair: `2579ac0acf70465a3902a8317ff241129b9b550e`.\n- Owner: `packages/offline-transactions/tests/leadership-replay.property.test.ts`.\n\n| ID | Review claim | Verdict and action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| F0 | The production rejection and rollback path is sound. | Confirmed; already fixed before this review. The original-guard mutant and five admission cases are recorded above. | Keep the public settlement, outbox, provider-call, and optimistic-row checks in the leadership oracle. |\n| F1 | The retained-leader provider-call count can run after storage becomes visible but before `executor.execute()` starts. | Confirmed; fixed in the reviewed executable head. | The oracle now separates the stored-record checkpoint from transaction settlement. |\n| F2 | Comparing the manual `commit()` and `isPersisted.promise` errors by identity overconstrains the contract; check only their class. | Refuted as a test flaw; retain the identity assertion. | `packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts` explicitly owns the same-instance error law for the underlying transaction. The offline manual path checks that its wrapper preserves it. |\n\nA controlled storage wrapper wrote the retained-leader record, signaled that it\nwas visible, and held its `set()` completion. On the starting code, the old\nprovider-count assertion failed at its original checkpoint: expected one call,\nobserved zero. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout or setup failure.\nAfter the repair, the same held-write case observed a pending caller, one stored\nrecord, and zero provider calls. It then released the write, awaited public\nsettlement, and observed one provider call and fulfillment. Clearing the\nprovider-call recorder after settlement made the moved assertion fail, proving\nthat the later check still rejects a missing call. The recorder corruption\nwas removed; the controlled hold remains in the oracle.\n\nThe `commit()`/`isPersisted.promise` identity law is explicit in\n`packages/db/tests/transactions.test.ts` and in the core transaction's original\nerror rethrow. The offline wrapper rethrows the same error. A temporary wrapper\nthat threw a fresh `NonRetriableError` with the same message made the manual\noracle's identity assertion fail at the intended checkpoint. Replacing that\nassertion with the proposed class-only check let the mutant pass. Both temporary\nchanges were removed; the core identity test passed on this branch. The review\ncorrectly spotted a dependence on identity, but that dependence protects an\nestablished behavior.\n\nThe changed executable owner passed its focused held-write case and the full\noffline package suite: 207 tests in 17 files. Package typecheck, changed-file\nPrettier, `git diff --check`, and the commit hook's ESLint task passed. The\naddition retains the admission law and bounded leadership-loss scope. In particular,\nORC-005 now names the stored-record and settled-call checkpoints separately;\nORC-006 has the controlled old-order failure and missing-call control;\nORC-010 releases the held write during cleanup; ORC-012 records the exact\nexecutable head. The new hold keeps leadership throughout the write. Leadership\nloss or regain across a held write and the IndexedDB browser composition remain\nwith the owners in the coverage map; this review does not close those cells.\n"}
